{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/1449591-just-ran-my-first-session-of-p-tc-and-i-got-a-bunch-of-questions","id":1449591,"title":"Just ran my first session of P:tC, and I got a bunch of questions","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Promethean: The Created"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-05-25T16:09:23-07:00","id":1449591,"title":"Just ran my first session of P:tC, and I got a bunch of questions","content":"Hello everyone,<br /> <br /> As someone once wisely said: &quot;The Map is not the Territory&quot;, and having read the rulebook, and now played the first session of my first P:tC campaign, I do have a few questions I would like your assistance with.<br /> <br /> I apologize for the long post, and for the length of some questions, but I didn't want to miss context.<br /> <br /> Some context for the campaign:<br /> <br /> <b>The Campaign;</b><br /> I was drawn to the game due to the inbuilt story about the Pilgrimmage, which I find very interesting both in concept and rule-book execution, and I wanted to keep this as the focal point of the campaign, with other supernaturals present, but not necessarily having a spotlight (unless the players seek them out).<br /> To make the Pilgrimmage central to the campaign, I have made the game set in Denmark (where we all live) in the years 2010-2020, with each session planned to take one ingame year. I also placed 9 Athanors at 9 cities in Denmark, each designating that the area is tightly bound to a virtue and that they will be able to find this if they look around. I have done this by creating 10 NPCs for each location that has some abstract relationship with that virtue. 5 of them that lacks the virtue and thus suffers drawbacks, and 5 who has the virtue in excess leading to other issues for them. This was to give the players some concrete people to assist/hinder (and to act as a reflection for the PCs themselves)<br /> <br /> The first session took place in our own hometown, where they found it to be connected to Dilligence (defined as &quot;The will to take action upon or against the world&quot;), which I found a fitting starting virtue for the Pilgrimmage. After the session however, we had just barely made it to end of January - not quite the full year. But I find that this is alright. They are just starting out finding their characters and establishing a status quo, which makes it easier to skip longer bits of time later.<br /> <br /> <b>The players;</b><ul><li><i>Aberdeen-Angus Black</i>. Tammuz created not for manual labor, but instead by a shrewd politician who needed a ruthless and loyal yes-man to write speeches, as well as commit dirty work against his political rivals. After the politician died suddenly (acute heart disease most likely), Aberdeen was left without a cause, and having burned all bridges with other in the same field, he now seeks a true companion, who will like him without having need of his function. I, as a GM, love this character concept. Aberdeen is starting out as a Aurum - Follower, having aligned himself with a political party, and is currently trying to curry favor with a passionate, but xenophobic single mom blaming immigrants for her kids being &quot;set back&quot; - whatever that actually means. (the woman represents part of the &quot;Too much diligence - willing to do anything to get her kids what they 'deserve'&quot;)</li> <li><i>Ali DiLaurentis. </i>A Galateid made from a popular Texan cheerleader killed by a jealous unpopular girl, who became the demiurge. Through their college years, Ali was used as a tool to climb the social ladder - and once she reached the top, the Galateid was discarded in order to not overshadow her master. Left without a purpose and any relations, Ali was inspired by the friendships she couldn't break on her masters command to find someone who would like her the same way. Starting on the path of Aurum - Companion, Ali infiltrated a high school and &quot;befriended&quot; 3 students, Anita, Henning and Ronnie, and tries to use them as an entrance to gain a social life. She has taken a liking to Ronnie, who is clearly being abused and belittled by his &quot;friends&quot;, but unwilling to stand up for himself. (Ronnie represents &quot;Too little Dilligence - unwilling to leave toxic friendships&quot;)</li> <li><i>Amenuel. </i>A wax statue of Amelia Earhart brought to life. As an Unfleshed, she currently seeks a purpose besides impersonating someone famous, she currently seeks the ability to fit in and also find out who she is (though the player has made clear that second goal is a fiction by the character, as she would sacrifice any personality she has, if just she could fit in). Amenuel is starting as a Plumbum - Chronicler, and is currently seeking information of any Prometheans visiting the city before them - which isn't going well. Amenuel ended the session in the local archives, and though the session had to end before the conversation could fully realised, she has met Sonja, a woman working the archives who doubts her own abilities and thus isn't willing to seek new experiences. (Sonja represents &quot;Too little diligence - unwilling to try new, due to her self-doubt&quot;)</li> <li><i>Thing Two. </i>A Frankenstein made in a lab, that then showcased their scientific marvel until disquiet set in, at which point they very gave him the boot without any assistance. He has decided to become successful no matter the cost in order to show up his creators. He is willing to sacrifice anyone and everything in order to achieve success. Thing Two is starting on the Path of Stannum as a Savage. He has decided to join every sports club he could and antagonize everyone both by being way too competitive and being abusive towards those around him. He has <i>met</i> a few of my planned NPCs, but he has immediately made himself antagonistic towards them, before learning to know any of them, so I count that as him haven't connected to any yet.</li> </ul><br /> So after that context; here are my questions: <ol class=\"decimal\"><li>What would you consider the most important parts of the rules to teach the players? It's not an uncomplicated game and we're all new to it, and I felt like I always hadn't explained everything well enough</li> <li>How often should disquiet rolls happen? I was surprised by how often they happened in my session, just following the book guidelines on page 173 of the rulebook.</li> <li>Is a Promethean aware that they have inflicted disquiet on a person? I narrated some fairly obvious behaviors for characters with the disquieted condition, that probably made it easy to guess for the players, but are the PCs actually aware of another person in disquiet?</li> <li>Is there an easy mnemonic or device to remember when to do Disquiet, Wasteland and Torment rolls? I found myself constantly consulting the book and self-made reminder cards to find out, and that took away game time.</li> <li>How often should modifiers apply to rolls? Since (to my knowledge) dramatic failures can only happen on a chance die, they don't seem to be able to happen that often unless dice penalties are applied often. We experienced a few in this session as our Unfleshed was not equipped to make the investigations she had set herself up to making, but otherwise not.</li> <li>How many Beats are typically awarded during one session? I gave out some, but it wasn't that many, and I felt that their distribution was a bit lopsided among players.</li> <li>A refinement specific one; what exactly is the difference between the Outcast and the Savage roles (both in Stannum)? I had read the Savage as being one who sought out to bring pain to others to somehow benefit from it himself, but the example given on page 183 (&quot;Breaking up or terrorizing a community&quot;) doesn't seem to support this. Thing Two seemed to just antagonize everyone on sight to, in his own words, &quot;see how far he can push them before they choose violence&quot; - a point he probably would have reached if not for the timely intervention of Ali. If I am to give some in-game hints for steering him to a fuller lesson I should probably be completely clear about the direction and intention of the Role.</li> <li>Pilgrimage question: I have defined my players pilgrimages, letting them choose their starting one, and informing of the second role for free. Would it be fair to include a role on their pilgrimages that run directly counter to goals - as a sort of way of teaching them not to do what they want? Example, having Ali, who's looking for a true friend, having to go through the Savage role in Stannum to showcase how not to be a friend; or having the ambitious Thing Two go through Aes, to show how cooporation can also help him forward? Or is this considered a dick move among storytellers?</li> <li>Pilgrimage question: How many milestones should one pre-plan for a pilgrimage? I had made chosen the roles for each when I made the pilgrimage (with option for changing unknown parts as pr question 8), but this led me to struggle a bit to find milestones during play. Is it suggested to have all set milestones planned as early as possible?</li> <li>I have asked this question on a different forum, but didn't get any clear answer; how important are antagonists for P:tC? I have planned a bunch of optional ones, but reading through the book it does seem to highlight a higher number of enemies than I envisioned planning the campaign.</li> </ol><br /> <br /> I thank very much in advance, and once again I apologize for the length of the post.<br /> <br /> - Fibecker","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26847-fibecker","name":"Fibecker","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-25T19:34:18-07:00","id":1449606,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Fibecker</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/1449591-just-ran-my-first-session-of-p-tc-and-i-got-a-bunch-of-questions#post1449591\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hello everyone,<br /> <ol class=\"decimal\"><li>What would you consider the most important parts of the rules to teach the players? It's not an uncomplicated game and we're all new to it, and I felt like I always hadn't explained everything well enough</li> <li>How often should disquiet rolls happen? I was surprised by how often they happened in my session, just following the book guidelines on page 173 of the rulebook.</li> <li>Is a Promethean aware that they have inflicted disquiet on a person? I narrated some fairly obvious behaviors for characters with the disquieted condition, that probably made it easy to guess for the players, but are the PCs actually aware of another person in disquiet?</li> <li>Is there an easy mnemonic or device to remember when to do Disquiet, Wasteland and Torment rolls? I found myself constantly consulting the book and self-made reminder cards to find out, and that took away game time.</li> <li>How often should modifiers apply to rolls? Since (to my knowledge) dramatic failures can only happen on a chance die, they don't seem to be able to happen that often unless dice penalties are applied often. We experienced a few in this session as our Unfleshed was not equipped to make the investigations she had set herself up to making, but otherwise not.</li> <li>How many Beats are typically awarded during one session? I gave out some, but it wasn't that many, and I felt that their distribution was a bit lopsided among players.</li> <li>A refinement specific one; what exactly is the difference between the Outcast and the Savage roles (both in Stannum)? I had read the Savage as being one who sought out to bring pain to others to somehow benefit from it himself, but the example given on page 183 (&quot;Breaking up or terrorizing a community&quot;) doesn't seem to support this. Thing Two seemed to just antagonize everyone on sight to, in his own words, &quot;see how far he can push them before they choose violence&quot; - a point he probably would have reached if not for the timely intervention of Ali. If I am to give some in-game hints for steering him to a fuller lesson I should probably be completely clear about the direction and intention of the Role.</li> <li>Pilgrimage question: I have defined my players pilgrimages, letting them choose their starting one, and informing of the second role for free. Would it be fair to include a role on their pilgrimages that run directly counter to goals - as a sort of way of teaching them not to do what they want? Example, having Ali, who's looking for a true friend, having to go through the Savage role in Stannum to showcase how not to be a friend; or having the ambitious Thing Two go through Aes, to show how cooporation can also help him forward? Or is this considered a dick move among storytellers?</li> <li>Pilgrimage question: How many milestones should one pre-plan for a pilgrimage? I had made chosen the roles for each when I made the pilgrimage (with option for changing unknown parts as pr question 8), but this led me to struggle a bit to find milestones during play. Is it suggested to have all set milestones planned as early as possible?</li> <li>I have asked this question on a different forum, but didn't get any clear answer; how important are antagonists for P:tC? I have planned a bunch of optional ones, but reading through the book it does seem to highlight a higher number of enemies than I envisioned planning the campaign.</li> </ol><br /> <br /> <br /> I thank very much in advance, and once again I apologize for the length of the post.<br /> <br /> - Fibecker</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm only really able to answer a few of these, but I'll try my best!<br /> <br /> 1. They should know the basic Promethean capabilities, as well as what their individual Transmutations can do. They don't need to know them by heart, but make sure they have a sense of what their characters can do.<br /> 2. The rule of thumb that worked for the game I was in had so anything <i>significant </i>under those guidelines was a Disquiet trigger. Don't bother unless an NPC becomes a focus in the scene or the story, or if it isn't particularly dramatic to call for it.<br /> 3. Inherently, I don't think so? But they probably notice pretty fast.<br /> 4. Index cards might help. Promethean has a lot of moving parts, even compared to <b>Mage </b>(which is more self-working, arguably), so it might be a good idea to just have some crib note on hand.<br /> 5. Modifiers are really a Storyteller-specific choice. I tend to be pretty light with them, but a lot of STs will modify almost every roll. Do what works best for you. Also, Dramatic Failures can apply as a player choice (for a Beat). If you fail, you can opt to downgrade it to a dramatic failure. Most dramatic failures in CofD Second Edition are self-inflicted.<br /> 6. I find three Beats for a session is good, but one is also appropriate if you want your players to work harder for their EXP. You can also group Beats as a pool to distribute evenly among the troupe if you're finding things are a bit lopsided.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-31T19:35:06-07:00","id":1450536,"title":"","content":"You probably should plan on multiple sessions to cover a year. Think of the years as Arcs rather than Chapters, which will allow more decompressed storytelling. I suspect you are trying to cram too much into your planned 9 sessions -- You've gone and made 9 plot coupons spread across the land, which doesn't give people a whole lot of time to interact with the NPCs you've developed and get to know them in any meaningful way before the group moves on to the next location.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>When the cat's a Stray, the mice will pray...</i>","edited":{"userid":25851,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25851-thestray7","user":"TheStray7","date":". 05-31-2021, 10:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25851-thestray7","name":"TheStray7","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25851_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"176"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-31T20:55:32-07:00","id":1450543,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Fibecker</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/1449591-just-ran-my-first-session-of-p-tc-and-i-got-a-bunch-of-questions#post1449591\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><ol class=\"decimal\"><li>What would you consider the most important parts of the rules to teach the players? It's not an uncomplicated game and we're all new to it, and I felt like I always hadn't explained everything well enough</li> <li>How often should disquiet rolls happen? I was surprised by how often they happened in my session, just following the book guidelines on page 173 of the rulebook.</li> <li>Is a Promethean aware that they have inflicted disquiet on a person? I narrated some fairly obvious behaviors for characters with the disquieted condition, that probably made it easy to guess for the players, but are the PCs actually aware of another person in disquiet?</li> <li>Is there an easy mnemonic or device to remember when to do Disquiet, Wasteland and Torment rolls? I found myself constantly consulting the book and self-made reminder cards to find out, and that took away game time.</li> <li>How often should modifiers apply to rolls? Since (to my knowledge) dramatic failures can only happen on a chance die, they don't seem to be able to happen that often unless dice penalties are applied often. We experienced a few in this session as our Unfleshed was not equipped to make the investigations she had set herself up to making, but otherwise not.</li> <li>How many Beats are typically awarded during one session? I gave out some, but it wasn't that many, and I felt that their distribution was a bit lopsided among players.</li> <li>A refinement specific one; what exactly is the difference between the Outcast and the Savage roles (both in Stannum)? I had read the Savage as being one who sought out to bring pain to others to somehow benefit from it himself, but the example given on page 183 (&quot;Breaking up or terrorizing a community&quot;) doesn't seem to support this. Thing Two seemed to just antagonize everyone on sight to, in his own words, &quot;see how far he can push them before they choose violence&quot; - a point he probably would have reached if not for the timely intervention of Ali. If I am to give some in-game hints for steering him to a fuller lesson I should probably be completely clear about the direction and intention of the Role.</li> <li>Pilgrimage question: I have defined my players pilgrimages, letting them choose their starting one, and informing of the second role for free. Would it be fair to include a role on their pilgrimages that run directly counter to goals - as a sort of way of teaching them not to do what they want? Example, having Ali, who's looking for a true friend, having to go through the Savage role in Stannum to showcase how not to be a friend; or having the ambitious Thing Two go through Aes, to show how cooporation can also help him forward? Or is this considered a dick move among storytellers?</li> <li>Pilgrimage question: How many milestones should one pre-plan for a pilgrimage? I had made chosen the roles for each when I made the pilgrimage (with option for changing unknown parts as pr question 8), but this led me to struggle a bit to find milestones during play. Is it suggested to have all set milestones planned as early as possible?</li> <li>I have asked this question on a different forum, but didn't get any clear answer; how important are antagonists for P:tC? I have planned a bunch of optional ones, but reading through the book it does seem to highlight a higher number of enemies than I envisioned planning the campaign.</li> </ol><br /> <br /> I thank very much in advance, and once again I apologize for the length of the post.<br /> <br /> - Fibecker</div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry to double post, but I thought I'd take a crack at some of these questions.<br /> <br /> 1. I'll second Yossarin here. Players should have a basic grasp of the fundamentals. Prometheans have a lot going on, and unlike, say, vampires, they don't have quite as big a stake in the cultural memory, so knowing what they can do is a plus. This is another reason I recommend planning for more sessions than you've got right now. It takes time for this stuff to sink in.<br /> <br /> 2. I, too, tend to roll for it only when there'd be some significant risk. Prometheans don't cause a lot of Disquiet at low Azoth levels, but that scales up as players get more powerful.<br /> <br /> 3. As I've played it, they can recognize the signs of Disquiet, but it's not an innate sense. Just a recognition of a trend. It's probably wise to use Empathy to detect Disquiet.<br /> <br /> 4. Disquiet is &quot;When I creep you out.&quot; It's when significant social details are exchanged, or a Promethean flares their disfigurements while using powers. Torment is &quot;When I seriously fucked up.&quot; It triggers from fire and major mistakes on the Pilgrimmage, plus gaining all your Willpower back by using Torment instead of Elpis. Wastelands are &quot;When shit has seriously gone tits up.&quot; It only happens when Promethans use a ton of Pyros at once, a bunch of Pandorans awake, a Promethean comes back from the dead, a Promethean is created, or when a Universal Milestone is achieved (this last is caused by something positive happening -- the rest are generally very negative).<br /> <br /> 5. It varies, based on how you want to do rolling. The more modifiers you use, the slower things tend to go as people calculate die pools. As Yossarin pointed out, most Dramatic Failures are self-inflicted for Beats.<br /> <br /> 6. This is only at my table, but I aim for around 3-5 Beats per character per session, plus 1-3 Vitriol Beats per character per session. To match this, I tend to lean into awarding Beats for good roleplaying or players sticking their characters into awkward situations. This is specific to what I want to get out of the game, though. If you want to, you can have Beats be a communal resources instead of an individual one -- each Beat the character earns goes into a communal pot and split evenly among the PCs (adding in Beats as necessary for round numbers). That's not my thing, but It might be yours. Talk with your players about whether they feel the same lopsidedness you do.<br /> <br /> 7. Outcasts hang back, Savages actively stir things up.<br /> <br /> 8. It's my personal opinion, but do not ever do this without discussing it with the players first. <i>Never force a significant change on a PC without the player's input</i>. You want to be on the same page as the player, otherwise it looks like you're being arbitrary and putting your desires for what you want to see as a Storyteller ahead of what the player wants to do with their character. The character may balk at having to go into an antithetical Role to progress themselves, but the player themselves should be 100% on board. If you want to see <i>why</i> you should never force things on the player, you need only to look at any entry on <a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/dndhorrorstories/\" target=\"_blank\">this forum</a>. It will show you real quick how unplanned and unwanted character changes can sour not just a session, but a whole game.<br /> <br /> 9. <i>Never force yourself to create more than you must</i>. Write this on the inside of your screen if you need to. Don't force yourself to prepare more things than you think you'll need for the next few sessions -- you might be tempted to lock in certain changes even when your Chronicle is veering in a different direction than the one you planned, and try to railroad things back to where you think they ought to go. Try to have the game situations dictate events, rather than specific plot points. A plot is &quot;First X happens, then Y happens, leading to Z outcome.&quot; A situation is &quot;Thing X happens, causing NPCs Q, R, and S to do Z in response, with goals of D, F, G.&quot; A plot is prescriptive, while a situation is reactive. Situations work better in PRGs because your players can and frequently will do things you did NOT expect.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>When the cat's a Stray, the mice will pray...</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25851-thestray7","name":"TheStray7","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25851_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"176"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-01T06:23:46-07:00","id":1450582,"title":"","content":"Thank you both so much! Lot of great info here! Thanks a bunch<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheStray7</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/1449591-just-ran-my-first-session-of-p-tc-and-i-got-a-bunch-of-questions?p=1450543#post1450543\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You probably should plan on multiple sessions to cover a year. Think of the years as Arcs rather than Chapters, which will allow more decompressed storytelling. I suspect you are trying to cram too much into your planned 9 sessions -- You've gone and made 9 plot coupons spread across the land, which doesn't give people a whole lot of time to interact with the NPCs you've developed and get to know them in any meaningful way before the group moves on to the next location.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I figured as much. I do quite like the campaign set up, and I don't mind having it be more sessions than the planned 10, it just seemed like an enticing number of games. I will replan my sessions - but since I already did so much planning, that probably won't require that much extra work<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yossarian</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. They should know the basic Promethean capabilities, as well as what their individual Transmutations can do. They don't need to know them by heart, but make sure they have a sense of what their characters can do.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheStray7</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/1449591-just-ran-my-first-session-of-p-tc-and-i-got-a-bunch-of-questions?p=1450543#post1450543\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. I'll second Yossarin here. Players should have a basic grasp of the fundamentals. Prometheans have a lot going on, and unlike, say, vampires, they don't have quite as big a stake in the cultural memory, so knowing what they can do is a plus. This is another reason I recommend planning for more sessions than you've got right now. It takes time for this stuff to sink in.</div> </div> </div> </div>They do have a lot going on, which is why I found it a little difficult to concisely explain it all quickly. I don't mind looking things up, but while I like the games, I think CofD rulebooks could use a bit of revision in layout. Some information about the same topic can seem to be scattered about several places in the book. I'm gonna make some more flash cards to remind myself - even if it takes some extra work. Who knows, maybe someone else can benefit from them, when I have made them<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yossarian</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">2. The rule of thumb that worked for the game I was in had so anything <i>significant </i>under those guidelines was a Disquiet trigger. Don't bother unless an NPC becomes a focus in the scene or the story, or if it isn't particularly dramatic to call for it.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheStray7</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/1449591-just-ran-my-first-session-of-p-tc-and-i-got-a-bunch-of-questions?p=1450543#post1450543\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2. I, too, tend to roll for it only when there'd be some significant risk. Prometheans don't cause a lot of Disquiet at low Azoth levels, but that scales up as players get more powerful.</div> </div> </div> </div>I just followed the guidelines, one of which being &quot;superficial social contact with a Promethean for an entire scene&quot;. As my players used their scenes to deliberately talk to people, this seemed quite often. I got a bit worried, Ali infiltrated a high school and sat in on a class, talking with the teacher, and participating in a group assignment - doesn't this technically count as &quot;superficial social contact with a Promethean&quot; for everyone in the class. I decided just to tone it down to those she had longer conversations with, so it seems like that's common from your responses<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yossarian</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">3. Inherently, I don't think so? But they probably notice pretty fast.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheStray7</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/1449591-just-ran-my-first-session-of-p-tc-and-i-got-a-bunch-of-questions?p=1450543#post1450543\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">3. As I've played it, they can recognize the signs of Disquiet, but it's not an innate sense. Just a recognition of a trend. It's probably wise to use Empathy to detect Disquiet.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's how I played it too, so that fits<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yossarian</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">4. Index cards might help. Promethean has a lot of moving parts, even compared to <b>Mage </b>(which is more self-working, arguably), so it might be a good idea to just have some crib note on hand.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheStray7</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/1449591-just-ran-my-first-session-of-p-tc-and-i-got-a-bunch-of-questions?p=1450543#post1450543\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">4. Disquiet is &quot;When I creep you out.&quot; It's when significant social details are exchanged, or a Promethean flares their disfigurements while using powers. Torment is &quot;When I seriously fucked up.&quot; It triggers from fire and major mistakes on the Pilgrimmage, plus gaining all your Willpower back by using Torment instead of Elpis. Wastelands are &quot;When shit has seriously gone tits up.&quot; It only happens when Promethans use a ton of Pyros at once, a bunch of Pandorans awake, a Promethean comes back from the dead, a Promethean is created, or when a Universal Milestone is achieved (this last is caused by something positive happening -- the rest are generally very negative).</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that's what I noticed. I'm gonna make some more flash-cards for the players<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yossarian</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">5. Modifiers are really a Storyteller-specific choice. I tend to be pretty light with them, but a lot of STs will modify almost every roll. Do what works best for you. Also, Dramatic Failures can apply as a player choice (for a Beat). If you fail, you can opt to downgrade it to a dramatic failure. Most dramatic failures in CofD Second Edition are self-inflicted.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheStray7</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/1449591-just-ran-my-first-session-of-p-tc-and-i-got-a-bunch-of-questions?p=1450543#post1450543\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">5. It varies, based on how you want to do rolling. The more modifiers you use, the slower things tend to go as people calculate die pools. As Yossarin pointed out, most Dramatic Failures are self-inflicted for Beats.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good to know. I was unsure if more modifiers were expected than I was currently aware of!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yossarian</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">6. I find three Beats for a session is good, but one is also appropriate if you want your players to work harder for their EXP. You can also group Beats as a pool to distribute evenly among the troupe if you're finding things are a bit lopsided.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheStray7</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/1449591-just-ran-my-first-session-of-p-tc-and-i-got-a-bunch-of-questions?p=1450543#post1450543\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">6. This is only at my table, but I aim for around 3-5 Beats per character per session, plus 1-3 Vitriol Beats per character per session. To match this, I tend to lean into awarding Beats for good roleplaying or players sticking their characters into awkward situations. This is specific to what I want to get out of the game, though. If you want to, you can have Beats be a communal resources instead of an individual one -- each Beat the character earns goes into a communal pot and split evenly among the PCs (adding in Beats as necessary for round numbers). That's not my thing, but It might be yours. Talk with your players about whether they feel the same lopsidedness you do.</div> </div> </div> </div>We've only had one session so far, but I noticed how some players only got one, while at least one player got four, so that felt a little off. I think I should add more for roleplaying into it, but my own inexperience with the system made me a bit rule-focused, and forgot to add for that. I will do so in future sessions!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheStray7</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/1449591-just-ran-my-first-session-of-p-tc-and-i-got-a-bunch-of-questions?p=1450543#post1450543\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">7. Outcasts hang back, Savages actively stir things up.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay, then my player was way closer than I was. He certainly was stirring things up - very much to his own detriment. I will just have a chat with him about the &quot;lesson&quot; his character thinks he is about to learn, so I can plant proper seeding! Thanks, I was quite confused from the text.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheStray7</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/1449591-just-ran-my-first-session-of-p-tc-and-i-got-a-bunch-of-questions?p=1450543#post1450543\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">8. It's my personal opinion, but do not ever do this without discussing it with the players first. <i>Never force a significant change on a PC without the player's input</i>. You want to be on the same page as the player, otherwise it looks like you're being arbitrary and putting your desires for what you want to see as a Storyteller ahead of what the player wants to do with their character. The character may balk at having to go into an antithetical Role to progress themselves, but the player themselves should be 100% on board. If you want to see <i>why</i> you should never force things on the player, you need only to look at any entry on <a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/dndhorrorstories/\" target=\"_blank\">this forum</a>. It will show you real quick how unplanned and unwanted character changes can sour not just a session, but a whole game.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, this is right. These were also some of the thoughts in the back of my mind. I actually Didn't include it at first, but then I noticed how their Pilgrimages seemed kinda one-dimensional. Ali who want's real friends spent a lot of time in Aurum and Aes, which is fine, but it seemed almost impossible to have her even enter something like Stannum or Ferrum, which is why I got the idea of Antithetical teachings. In the game, I guess that's kinda what Thing Two is doing by going around pissing everyone off.<br /> <br /> Having this talk with the players may of course reveal some of the mystery, but I can work on that. Thanks you for the input<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheStray7</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/1449591-just-ran-my-first-session-of-p-tc-and-i-got-a-bunch-of-questions?p=1450543#post1450543\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">9. <i>Never force yourself to create more than you must</i>. Write this on the inside of your screen if you need to. Don't force yourself to prepare more things than you think you'll need for the next few sessions -- you might be tempted to lock in certain changes even when your Chronicle is veering in a different direction than the one you planned, and try to railroad things back to where you think they ought to go. Try to have the game situations dictate events, rather than specific plot points. A plot is &quot;First X happens, then Y happens, leading to Z outcome.&quot; A situation is &quot;Thing X happens, causing NPCs Q, R, and S to do Z in response, with goals of D, F, G.&quot; A plot is prescriptive, while a situation is reactive. Situations work better in PRGs because your players can and frequently will do things you did NOT expect.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am well aware of this from my now fairly long RPG-playing, but I was interested in how others do it in regards to P:tC as the game kinda seemed to invite that. I havn't planned anything yet except some flexible NPCs they can meet each place, and they work better as situations than specific goals. Good to know<br /> <br /> Thank you very much, both of you","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26847-fibecker","name":"Fibecker","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-01T21:53:24-07:00","id":1450663,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Fibecker</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/1449591-just-ran-my-first-session-of-p-tc-and-i-got-a-bunch-of-questions?p=1450582#post1450582\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They do have a lot going on, which is why I found it a little difficult to concisely explain it all quickly. I don't mind looking things up, but while I like the games, I think CofD rulebooks could use a bit of revision in layout. Some information about the same topic can seem to be scattered about several places in the book. I'm gonna make some more flash cards to remind myself - even if it takes some extra work. Who knows, maybe someone else can benefit from them, when I have made them</div> </div> </div> </div>You and me both, friend. You and me both. <b>Tangent</b>: I think the best laid-out book is the 2e Geist book, as that takes pains to separate out fluff and crunch (and bullet-points the crunch for easier reading) and does a nice amount of cross-checking. It's not perfect, but it's an improvement over the layout of previous titles, having benefited from hindsight. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We've only had one session so far, but I noticed how some players only got one, while at least one player got four, so that felt a little off. I think I should add more for roleplaying into it, but my own inexperience with the system made me a bit rule-focused, and forgot to add for that. I will do so in future sessions! </div> </div> </div>Is that including the free Beat players get each session? because yeah, I can see how that could get lopsided. <br /> <br /> Okay, then my player was way closer than I was. He certainly was stirring things up - very much to his own detriment. I will just have a chat with him about the &quot;lesson&quot; his character thinks he is about to learn, so I can plant proper seeding! Thanks, I was quite confused from the text.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yeah, this is right. These were also some of the thoughts in the back of my mind. I actually Didn't include it at first, but then I noticed how their Pilgrimages seemed kinda one-dimensional. Ali who want's real friends spent a lot of time in Aurum and Aes, which is fine, but it seemed almost impossible to have her even enter something like Stannum or Ferrum, which is why I got the idea of Antithetical teachings. In the game, I guess that's kinda what Thing Two is doing by going around pissing everyone off.<br /> <br /> Having this talk with the players may of course reveal some of the mystery, but I can work on that. Thanks you for the input<br /> <br /> I am well aware of this from my now fairly long RPG-playing, but I was interested in how others do it in regards to P:tC as the game kinda seemed to invite that. I havn't planned anything yet except some flexible NPCs they can meet each place, and they work better as situations than specific goals. Good to know </div> </div> </div>I wasn't sure how much GMing experience you had with other systems, so if I come off as preachy or condescending, I apologize. But yeah, discuss Pilgrimages with the player, and see if you can offer some ideas. Prometheans are unique in that it's really easy for them to switch up their power sets by adopting a new Refinement, especially among the 5 Simple Refinements, so that's a strength you should play up. None of the other splats can say &quot;fuck it, I want to throw lightning around now&quot; like one of the Created can (by abandoning their current role to jump into Stannum).<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>When the cat's a Stray, the mice will pray...</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25851-thestray7","name":"TheStray7","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25851_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"176"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}